  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nWet pattern expected over the next 48 hours. Broad southwest\nflow ahead of an approaching upper level shortwave trough that\nreach the region for the weekend. Stationary boundary will \ndevelop just off the Maine coast today that will be a focus \narea for steady rain and potentially some localized heavy\ndownpours. Still enough spread in the guidance to support the \npotential that the outer islands and necks could be on the edge \nof the heavier rain axis this afternoon into Saturday morning. \nTrended up the rainfall amounts a bit for the first wave \ntomorrow closer to the main guidance as enough guidance has \ntrended just slightly westward again. A brief break in the rain \nis possible Saturday morning before the upper level approaches \nand captures some of the coastal moisture fetch. Its even \npossible a surface low pressure system will develop in the Gulf \nof Maine, increasing low level dynamics for a developing \ndeformation zone across the region Saturday night. This will be \nthe prime window for rainfall chances across the interior \nthrough midday Sunday as the developing surface and upper level \nfeatures move eastward by Sunday afternoon ending the widespread\nrainfall. Overall expecting precipitation amounts ranging from \nnear 2\" for Penobscot Bay Islands to only a quarter of an inch \nfor the CT River Valley.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAs the consolidated low moves northeast, \nit will slow and expand in size, keeping us on the periphery into next \nweek. This will help bring cooler temperatures back to the region as \nwell as additional light precipitation. The airmass will continue to \ncool aloft with 850 mb temperatures bottoming out at -1 to -5C by Monday \nevening. This should translate to daytime highs in the 40s across the \nmountains with 50s towards the interior and coast for much of the week. \nCyclonic flow continuing through midweek and a fair amount of cloud \ncover should keep lows on the milder side, likely in the 30s to low \n40s, with radiational cooling limited.\n\nWith cyclonic flow in place, with a series of hard-to-time waves will \nmove through the flow. This suggests shower chances for the first half \nof the week, driven by cold air aloft. More clarity on the \ntiming/impacts of each wave should become clear in the coming days. \nPattern recognition suggests the best odds for cloud cover and \nprecipitation to be across the White Mtns. and far interior portions of \nthe forecast area, with a lesser chance towards the coast. Freezing \nlevels just off the deck suggest wet snowflakes may mix in for northern \nzones, with light accumulations in the mountains possible.",
